      <ul>
        <li><strong>Claude got every question right,</strong> including the multi-traveler planning questions. If you're going to ask a free chatbot about your Schengen days, our results point to Claude.</li>
        <li><strong>20 of the 57 answers weren't fully right.</strong> 16 were wrong outright, and 4 were close or only reached the right date after first giving a wrong one.</li>
        <li><strong>The wrong answers sounded just as sure as the right ones.</strong> Nothing in the tone of an answer told you which kind you were reading.</li>
        <li><strong>Models are improving quickly.</strong> These results are a snapshot of October 2026, and the paid models we tried already did much better (see <a href="#methodology">Methodology</a>).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="why-this-happens">Why a wrong answer is easy to believe</h2>

      <p>
        This isn't a knock on any specific model, and the best of them are
        getting this right. The problem is what happens when one doesn't.
        A chatbot's wrong date arrives in the same confident, well-written
        paragraph as a right one, often with plausible-looking working
        attached. Checking it means redoing the arithmetic yourself: days
        across month boundaries, both entry and exit days counted, and a
        <em>rolling</em> 180-day window (not a fixed period — see
        <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window">our explainer
        on that</a>). That's exactly the work you were trying to avoid by
        asking. So in practice, most people don't check, and a wrong answer
        delivered with full confidence gets treated as fact.
      </p>

      <p>
        The wrong answers went both ways. Several assumed you have to wait
        for an old trip to clear the window completely before starting a
        new 90-day stay, which sounds cautious but pushed return dates
        back by up to three months. Others erred the dangerous way: Gemini
        told Anna she could stay until July 31, seven weeks past her real
        limit of June 11, and ChatGPT gave her one day too many. Either
        mistake is easy to believe when it's explained this confidently.
      </p>

