      <!-- ================= QUESTION 3 ================= -->
      <h2 id="q3">Question 3 — "On what date must [Person] exit?"</h2>

      <p>
        The mirror image of Question 2: dates are already booked for an
        entry, and the question is how long the trip can safely run. This
        is the question with the highest real-world stakes, since getting
        it wrong in the optimistic direction is exactly how someone ends up
        overstaying — see
        <a href="/blog/schengen-overstay-consequences">what actually happens
        if that occurs</a>. It also depends on getting
        <a href="/blog/does-your-entry-day-count-as-a-schengen-day">entry-day
        counting</a> right, which is a second, independent place for a model
        to be off by one.
      </p>

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Patricia entering Mar 1 (Scenario A)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Patricia holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. She has no Schengen Area travel history before this trip.

If Patricia enters the Schengen Area on March 1, 2025, what is the last date she is allowed to still be present in the Schengen Area before she must exit to stay compliant with the 90/180 rule? Give a specific date and briefly explain your reasoning, including whether entry and exit days themselves count toward the 90 days.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Both calculators: May 29, 2025.</p>
      <ChatbotAnswers id="q3-1" results={results} />

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Anna entering May 3 (Scenario B)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Anna holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. Her only prior Schengen Area travel: entered February 1, 2025, exited March 22, 2025 (50 days). No other history.

If Anna enters the Schengen Area again on May 3, 2025, what is the last date she is allowed to still be present in the Schengen Area before she must exit to stay compliant with the 90/180 rule? Give a specific date and briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Published answer: June 11, 2025 — <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window#case-3">see Anna's worked example</a>. She's capped well below 90 days because her earlier trip is still fully inside the window.</p>
      <ChatbotAnswers id="q3-2" results={results} />

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Marcus entering Jul 15 (Scenario B)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Marcus holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. His only prior Schengen Area travel: entered February 1, 2025, exited March 22, 2025 (50 days). No other history.

If Marcus enters the Schengen Area again on July 15, 2025, what is the last date he is allowed to still be present in the Schengen Area before he must exit to stay compliant with the 90/180 rule? Give a specific date and briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Published answer: October 12, 2025 — <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window#case-3">see Marcus's worked example</a>. This is the hardest of the four: his earlier trip ages out of the window <em>during</em> this stay, so his true maximum is much longer than his remaining allowance on July 15 alone suggests.</p>
      <ChatbotAnswers id="q3-3" results={results} />

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Jordan back with his bike, Apr 23, 2026 (Scenario C)</summary>
