      <!-- ================= QUESTION 5 ================= -->
      <h2 id="q5">Question 5 — Coordinating trips</h2>

      <p>
        Real travel plans rarely involve one person and one trip. Friends
        try to line up a week together, someone wants to join a friend
        who's already there, and weather moves a flight. Each of these
        questions means tracking several trips, and sometimes several
        people, at once. That's where a single wrong assumption about the
        window quietly breaks a plan that looked fine. These prompts are
        harder, and each one has a tempting shortcut that gives the wrong
        answer.
      </p>

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Anna, Marcus, and Ilker meet up for a week (Scenario B)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Anna, Marcus, and Ilker all hold passports that allow 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. In 2025 they took a trip together, entering the Schengen Area on February 1 and exiting on March 22. Since then:
- Anna returned on her own from May 3 to June 11, 2025.
- Marcus is in the Schengen Area from July 15 to October 12, 2025.
- Ilker is in the Schengen Area from August 1 to October 29, 2025.
None of them has any other Schengen Area travel.

Anna wants to fly back and spend a 7-day trip with Marcus and Ilker while both of them are still there. What is the earliest date the three of them could start that week together, without Anna overstaying the 90/180 rule at any point? Give a specific date and briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">Correct answer: August 1, 2025. *Both calculators answer July 31, which is when Anna herself can return; the three can't start together until Ilker arrives on August 1. Anna hits exactly 90 days on June 11, but her February days start aging out on July 31, and from then she gets one day back for every day she stays. She could return on July 31, so the limit is Ilker's arrival on August 1. A model that applies "90 days in, 90 days out" will push Anna to around September 10.</p>
      <ChatbotAnswers id="q5-1" results={results} />

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — the group joins Jordan in September (Scenarios B and C)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Four friends — Anna, Marcus, Ilker, and Olivia — all hold passports that allow 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. In 2025 they traveled together, entering the Schengen Area on February 1 and exiting on March 22. Their other Schengen Area travel, already taken or booked, is:
- Anna: May 3 – June 11, 2025, and a winter trip from October 15, 2025 to January 10, 2026.
- Marcus: July 15 – October 12, 2025.
- Ilker: August 1 – October 29, 2025.
- Olivia: September 27 – December 25, 2025.

Their friend Jordan will be in the Schengen Area from September 19 to 26, 2025, and all four want to spend that whole week with him. Can each of them join without breaking the 90/180 rule? For anyone who can't simply add that week, keep the start date of their booked trip and give the latest date they could end it instead. Give specific dates and briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">EuroVisaCalculator agrees; the official EU calculator couldn't answer this one. Marcus and Ilker can join with no changes, because they're already there that week and it's inside their booked trips. Anna can join, but her winter trip must end by January 4, 2026, six days early: she had only two spare days, and the September week stays in her window all winter. Olivia can join by arriving on September 19, but she must then leave by December 17, 2025, eight days early. Count a model as correct only if it gets all four right.</p>
      <ChatbotAnswers id="q5-2" results={results} />

      <details class="prompt-box">
        <summary>Prompt — Jordan extends Trip 3 because of the weather (Scenario C)</summary>
        <pre class="prompt-text">Jordan holds a passport that allows 90 days of visa-free travel in the Schengen Area within any rolling 180-day period. Jordan's Schengen Area travel so far is:
- Trip 1: March 14–21, 2025
- Trip 2: April 18 – July 2, 2025
- Trip 3: in the Schengen Area since September 19, 2025, booked to leave on September 26

Jordan has also booked these future trips:
- Trip 4: November 27 – December 30, 2025
- Trip 5: April 12–17, 2026
- Trip 6: April 23 – May 10, 2026

Bad weather has cancelled Jordan's flight, and he wants to stay longer on Trip 3. What is the latest date he can leave without overstaying the 90/180 rule, and without making any of his booked future trips non-compliant? Give a specific date and briefly explain your reasoning.</pre>
      </details>
      <p class="known-answer">EuroVisaCalculator: October 2, 2025, six extra days; the official EU calculator couldn't answer this one. The limit comes from the past, not the future: Trip 2's 76 days are still in the window, so on October 2 the window holds exactly 90. Staying until then doesn't affect any of the booked future trips. A model that only checks the future trips will let him stay far too long.</p>
      <ChatbotAnswers id="q5-3" results={results} />

