      <h2 id="bonus-prompts">Bonus prompts worth trying</h2>

      <p>
        A few more questions we considered but kept out of the main grid —
        either because they're diagnostic rather than a clean right/wrong
        check, or because they test something adjacent worth its own
        section:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Quick-status framing:</strong> "As of [date], how many
          Schengen days does [Person] have remaining?" — probably the single
          most common real phrasing; useful as a sanity check that ties
          back into every other answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The conceptual trap:</strong> "Marcus enters the Schengen
          Area on July 15, 2025 with 40 days of allowance remaining. Two
          weeks into his stay, his remaining allowance hasn't changed. Is
          that a bug?" A model that says yes has failed to understand
          <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window#case-3">the
          aging-out mechanism</a>, not just failed at arithmetic — worth its
          own comparison table if this post does well.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adjudicating disagreement:</strong> "Two calculators gave
          me different answers for the same dates: Calculator A says 12
          days remaining, Calculator B says 7. Which is more likely to be
          correct and why?" — a direct test of whether the model can reason
          about <em>why</em> tools disagree, tying into
          <a href="/blog/why-schengen-calculators-give-different-results">our
          first post in this series</a>, rather than just picking one at
          random.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deliberately underspecified:</strong> Ask any of the five
          core questions but omit whether entry/exit days count, to see
          which models ask a clarifying question versus silently assume
          an answer — the assumption itself is worth recording, since
          <a href="/blog/does-your-entry-day-count-as-a-schengen-day">it's
          the single biggest source of off-by-one errors</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="why-it-matters">Why this matters</h2>

      <p>
        A wrong answer from a chatbot doesn't come with a caveat, a
        confidence interval, or a link to its sources — it comes as a
        sentence that sounds exactly as certain as a right one. For
        anything else, that's a minor inconvenience. For this specific
        question, being off by even one day in the optimistic direction is
        the difference between a normal trip and an overstay, and
        <a href="/blog/schengen-overstay-consequences">the consequences of
        an overstay</a> range from an awkward conversation to an entry ban
        that follows you into future visa applications. It also shapes what
        happens at the border itself:
        <a href="/blog/why-border-control-gave-you-a-different-number">when
        an officer's number disagrees with yours</a>, being able to show a
        day-by-day timeline is a meaningfully different position to argue
        from than scrolling back through a chat.
      </p>

      <h2 id="why-evc">Why we still built a calculator</h2>

      <p>
        Even when a chatbot gets the date exactly right, the answer is a
        paragraph in one person's chat. You can't easily share it with the
        people you're traveling with, see everyone's days side by side, or
        update the plan when a flight moves without asking all over again.
        The official EU calculator is always accurate, and it's the
        reference we check every answer against. But it works out one
        traveler at a time, and it couldn't answer our two hardest planning
        questions at all. <a href="/app">EuroVisaCalculator</a> is built to
        make planning trips easier, for everyone on the trip:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>One shared plan.</strong> Your trips are saved in the link, with no account required, so everyone you send it to sees the same itinerary.</li>
        <li><strong>Multi-person by default.</strong> Every scenario in this post involves more than one traveler with different dates — <a href="/blog/schengen-calculator-couples">exactly the case a chat thread or a single-traveler calculator isn't built for</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>See every day, not just a final date.</strong> Each day counted toward your allowance is visible on the timeline, so you can check the answer rather than take it on trust.</li>
        <li><strong>Coverage beyond Schengen.</strong> We track 47 European countries and territories, not just the Schengen Area — useful the moment a trip includes the UK, Ireland, or a bilateral-agreement country.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <a href="/app">Try EuroVisaCalculator with your own dates</a> —
        or, if you want to see the exact mechanics one more time before you
        do, <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window">the rolling
        window post</a> these scenarios came from.
      </p>

